diagnosis_text:
The network is fully healthy. Direct inspection of every component in the reported failure path shows no fault.

Laptop2 (172.16.10.12) and Laptop3 (172.16.10.13) are both assigned addresses in the same subnet (172.16.10.0/24), their eth0 interfaces are UP and LOWER_UP, and neither host has any iptables rules in place. Switch1's bridge table shows that both laptop-facing ports — eth3 (Laptop2's cd_sw1_fa3_1) and eth4 (Laptop3's cd_sw1_fa4_1) — are members of the same bridge domain (br_vlan10) and are both in FORWARDING state. No STP blocking, no missing VLAN membership. Live ping tests run in both directions confirmed 0% packet loss with sub-millisecond round-trip times (4/4 packets received in each direction). ARP resolution is working: Laptop2 has Laptop3's MAC address marked REACHABLE, and Laptop3 has Laptop2's MAC address in its neighbor cache.

The root cause of the ticket is that the reported symptom does not correspond to any actual network fault — Laptop2 and Laptop3 can communicate normally in both directions at this time.

Corrective action: No configuration change is needed. The helpdesk team should ask the user to re-test file sharing and confirm whether the issue was transient (e.g., a momentary ARP miss or application-level timeout) rather than a persistent network fault. No switch port reconfiguration or restart is warranted.
